<PageLayout
  title="MC5R — the exocrine receptor"
  kicker="Receptor · Melanocortin 5 receptor"
  description="The least understood of the five, but with a clear signature role: regulating exocrine glands, most notably the sebaceous glands that oil skin and fur — and, it turns out, a hand in muscle fuel, fat cells and regulatory immunity."
  thread="The system's least-told chapter — the same signal at the body's surface glands, a quiet corner of the map still being read for the first time."
  updated="2026"
  about={[
    { name: 'Melanocortin 5 receptor', type: 'MedicalEntity', sameAs: ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Melanocortin_5_receptor'] },
  ]}
>
  <ReceptorFacts receptor={receptor} />

  <KeyTakeaways
    items={[
      'MC5R is the least understood of the five melanocortin receptors, but with a clear signature role in exocrine glands.',
      'Its best-defined job is regulating sebaceous secretion — the sebum and lipids that oil skin and fur; in human skin it marks the maturing, lipid-laden sebocyte.',
      'It is activated by α-MSH and ACTH, couples to Gs and cyclic AMP, and its gene is a compact single exon on chromosome 18.',
      'Beyond the glands it nudges peripheral fuel use — fat oxidation and glucose uptake in muscle, lipolysis in fat — and helps the immune system stand down after autoimmune attack.',
      'Blocking it shrinks sebaceous glands in human skin, which is why MC5R is watched for acne; no such drug has yet reached the clinic.',
    ]}
  />

  <p>
    MC5R is the melanocortin family's generalist. Its messenger RNA shows up in a
    strikingly wide range of tissues, which long made its core function hard to
    name. The answer came from removing it: the defining role of MC5R is the
    control of exocrine glands.<Cite n={1} /> What has emerged since is a receptor
    that reads the same melanocortin signal at several of the body's peripheral
    outlets at once — glands, fuel stores, and immune cells.
  </p>

  <Figure
    caption="The same melanocortin signal, read by MC5R, fans out to three kinds of peripheral work: the surface glands that are its signature role, fuel handling in muscle and fat, and a regulatory arm of the immune system."
    source="Schematic, after Chen et al. 1997, An et al. 2007, Enriori et al. 2016 and Lee & Taylor 2013."
  >
    <OneReceptorManyOutlets />
  </Figure>

  <h2>The sebum signal</h2>
  <p>
    When the Mc5r gene is knocked out in mice, the animals develop a severe defect
    in water repulsion and thermoregulation, traced to reduced production of
    sebaceous lipids — the oily secretion that waterproofs fur and skin. The
    knockout also impairs other exocrine tissues, including the Harderian,
    lacrimal, and preputial glands: the Harderian gland no longer makes its
    stress-regulated porphyrins, and the lacrimal gland loses its
    melanocortin-driven protein secretion. One receptor, in other words,
    coordinates secretion across several gland types.<Cite n={2} /> Mechanistically
    MC5R behaves like its relatives — coupling to Gs and raising cyclic AMP — with
    α-MSH and ACTH as agonists.<Cite n={3} />
  </p>

  <h2>The MC5R gene</h2>
  <p>
    The receptor was cloned in 1993 as the fifth and last member of the family, a
    325-amino-acid protein encoded by a single intronless exon on chromosome 18
    (18p11.21).<Cite n={4} /> Unlike <a href="/receptors/mc1r">MC1R</a>, whose
    variants rewrite hair and skin colour, or <a href="/receptors/mc4r">MC4R</a>,
    whose loss-of-function alleles are the commonest single-gene cause of obesity,
    MC5R has yielded no clean human phenotype. A systematic screen across diverse
    populations found one non-synonymous variant, Phe209Leu, that signals normally
    in the lab, and no MC5R mutations in people with acne, hidradenitis
    suppurativa, or sebaceous-gland tumours.<Cite n={5} /> The same study placed
    the receptor's expression in human skin: present in keratinocytes and in the
    epithelium of glands and hair follicles, absent from fibroblasts and
    melanocytes.<Cite n={5} /> MC5R is a receptor whose importance is written in
    what it does, not in what breaks when it varies.
  </p>

  <h2>A marker of the maturing sebocyte</h2>
  <p>
    The mouse story translates cleanly to human sebaceous glands, with a telling
    refinement. In human skin, MC5R is found only on differentiating, lipid-laden
    sebocytes — not on the basal, undifferentiated cells that give rise to them.
    Cultured human sebocytes show the same switch: no receptor until
    differentiation begins, then strong expression in cells packed with lipid
    granules.<Cite n={6} /> MC5R is, in effect, a marker of a sebocyte's maturity —
    the point at which it commits to making sebum — and the melanocortin signal
    that reaches it drives that lipid programme forward.
  </p>

  <h2>Turning sebum down</h2>
  <p>
    If MC5R pushes sebum up, blocking it should push sebum down, and the clearest
    test used human tissue. A small-molecule antagonist of MC1R and MC5R,
    JNJ-10229570, dose-dependently cut sebaceous-lipid production in cultured
    human sebocytes; applied topically to human skin grafted onto immunodeficient
    mice, it shrank the sebaceous glands, reduced sebum-specific lipids, and lowered
    a marker of sebaceous differentiation.<Cite n={7} /> That is the strongest
    evidence that MC5R is a workable lever on human sebum, and it is why the
    receptor is watched for acne and other sebum-driven conditions. It has not yet
    produced an approved drug, and the antagonist's MC1R activity is a reminder
    that dialling one receptor without touching its pigment-controlling cousin is
    part of the challenge — the same selectivity problem this site maps for{' '}
    <a href="/mc1r-selectivity">MC1R</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Fuel at the periphery</h2>
  <p>
    The central melanocortin receptors decide how much energy the body acquires
    and how it is spent (see <a href="/receptors/mc4r">MC4R</a> and{' '}
    <a href="/receptors/mc3r">MC3R</a>). MC5R appears to act at the far end of
    that chain, in the tissues that burn and store fuel. In skeletal muscle, α-MSH
    raises fatty-acid oxidation in a dose-dependent way through a cyclic
    AMP–protein kinase A–AMPK cascade, and silencing MC5R abolishes the
    effect.<Cite n={8} /> The same peptide also promotes glucose uptake into
    muscle through MC5R, which is part of why circulating α-MSH lowers blood
    glucose.<Cite n={9} /> In fat cells, α-MSH signalling through MC5R drives
    lipolysis — the release of stored triglyceride as glycerol and free fatty
    acids — via the lipases and perilipin that protein kinase A
    activates.<Cite n={10} /> Taken together these findings sketch a peripheral
    arm of the melanocortin system that mobilises and burns fuel, mirroring the
    central arm that curbs intake. The evidence is largely from mice and cell
    culture; how much it contributes to human metabolism is an open and
    interesting question.
  </p>

  <h2>Standing the immune system down</h2>
  <p>
    Beyond the sebaceous gland, MC5R turns up on immune cells — B and T
    lymphocytes among them — where melanocortin signalling helps regulate the
    inflammatory response.<Cite n={3} /> Its best-characterised immune job comes
    from the eye. After experimental autoimmune uveitis resolves in mice, the
    spleen holds a population of regulatory antigen-presenting cells that induce
    the FoxP3<sup>+</sup> regulatory T cells which protect against a second
    attack. That protection depends on MC5R: mice lacking it fail to expand those
    cells and lose the post-disease tolerance, with the adenosine A2A receptor on
    T cells completing the circuit.<Cite n={11} /> It places MC5R inside the
    melanocortin system's broad <a href="/inflammation">pro-resolving reach</a>,
    and it is one reason the receptor is studied as a target in autoimmune and
    inflammatory disease.
  </p>

  <h2>The receptor in three dimensions</h2>
  <p>
    MC5R was the last of the five to be seen. In 2023, cryo-EM structures of the
    human receptor bound to α-MSH and to the potent selective agonist PG-901,
    each coupled to Gs, showed the familiar family fold: the peptide's
    His-Phe-Arg-Trp motif in a U-shaped conformation, buried in a wide-open
    pocket.<Cite n={12} /> The comparison with MC3R in the same study is the
    useful part. γ-MSH's C-terminus reaches into an MC3R-specific groove that helps
    explain its preference for that subtype, whereas α-MSH's C-terminus barely
    touches MC5R at all — a hint that the receptor's selectivity is decided by
    subtler features of the pocket than the peptide tail. See the{' '}
    <a href="/structures">structural biology</a> page for the whole family and the{' '}
    <a href="/binding-matrix">binding matrix</a> for the affinities.
  </p>

  <h2>Why MC5R is watched</h2>
  <p>
    The therapeutic interest cuts several ways. In the skin, an MC5R antagonist
    that turns sebaceous output down is an appealing idea for acne; in the immune
    system, MC5R agonism is explored for a calming, resolving effect on
    inflammation; and in metabolism, its peripheral fuel-burning effects are being
    read for what they might add to the central appetite story.<Cite n={3} />
    None of this has produced an approved drug, and MC5R remains the least
    pinned-down of the five — but it reads as a genuine frontier rather than a
    closed chapter.
  </p>

  <p>
    For how MC5R fits the broader system, see the{' '}
    <a href="/system">overview pillar</a>; the related skin receptor is{' '}
    <a href="/receptors/mc1r">MC1R</a>.
  </p>

